      "Parigha is the nineteenth yoga. The word names an iron bar or bolt used to fasten a gate, and also a barrier or obstruction in a wider sense. The picture is of a closed door that will not open. Almanacs give Vishvakarma, the divine craftsman, as its deity, and lists its nature as inauspicious with the first half of the yoga being the difficult part.",
      "Unlike the full-day cautionary yogas, Parigha is treated in two halves. The opening half is linked with quarrels, obstacles and legal issues, and the second half is said to be workable. The things advised against are marriage, new ventures, court cases and property registration, along with travel in the first half. The usual suggestion is to use the first half for inner review and the second for routine tasks, which turns a restriction into a simple division of the day.",
      "The presence of Vishvakarma here is intriguing. He is the patron of artisans, tools and construction, so one might expect a constructive tone, yet the name of the yoga speaks of barriers. Readers sometimes reconcile the two by noting that a bolt is itself a craftsman's object. That is an interpretation, not a textual claim. Practices differ, and many people consult the panchang only for large decisions. If your family follows a specific almanac, defer to its verdict over a general description such as this one. Parigha comes after Variyana and before the benevolent Shiva yoga.",
