      "Siddha is the twenty-first yoga. As an adjective it means accomplished, perfected or established, and as a noun it names the perfected beings of Indian spiritual literature. The deity in most almanacs is Kartikeya, the commander of the divine army, and the nature is auspicious. A general who finishes campaigns is a fitting figurehead for a yoga about completion.",
      "What sets Siddha apart from its lookalike Siddhi is the direction of its emphasis. Siddhi is the one for beginnings; Siddha is described as a day for bringing pending matters to a close. The usual list includes finishing a project, sitting an exam, receiving a court verdict in your favour, marriage and mantra practice. The one thing to avoid is starting from scratch, and almanacs actually refer readers to Siddhi for that purpose. It is a neat division of labour between two near-homonyms.",
