      "Sadhya is the twenty-second yoga. The word comes from the root sadh, to accomplish, and means something that can be achieved or is fit to be accomplished. It also appears in older literature as a class of celestial beings. Savitr is listed as the deity, the Vedic solar impulse best known from the Gayatri mantra, and gives its nature as auspicious.",
      "The key idea is attainability with effort. Almanac users read it as a day when goals come within reach if pursued steadily. Goal-setting, ambitious launches, a sports event, filing for an election and submitting a tender bid are the examples. The one thing to avoid is a quick-result mindset, which seems fitting for a yoga whose name implies a task that can be done but not instantly. This is a pleasantly realistic note in a body of lore not always known for modesty.",
      "Savitr gives the yoga a morning character, and tradition suggests that offering water to the sun at dawn is a supportive practice. That ties the yoga to the daily habit of surya arghya common in many households. Sadhya follows Siddha and precedes Shubha, and with them it forms a trio of bright, goal-friendly yogas. Different almanacs mark the start and end at slightly different moments depending on the method and the place. Take the tradition as a gentle prompt to commit to a plan and keep at it, because the effort itself is what the name quietly insists upon.",
